  "text": "Here's the prototype actually running and the most important lesson it taught us, which is: don't chase perfection. Look at this. Even if a single apartment comes out right eighty percent of the time, the errors pile up across a dozen apartments, the odds the whole building is right drop to around SEVEN percent. That's just how complex systems go little errors compound, and perfect is off the table. But the fix is simple, at least in principle: don't make one design, make a whole bunch and filter for the good ones. The catch is that filtering means telling good architecture from bad automatically. And that's why everything from here on is about EVALUATION because in a system like this, being able to judge the result is the thing that makes it usable at all.",
